namespace Death {
//###==##====#=====--==~--~=~- --- -- -  -  -   -

/** @page amiga Building the project for Amiga systems
@brief Guide how to build, deploy and run Jazz² Resurrection on AmigaOS 3.x, AmigaOS 4.1 and MorphOS

@tableofcontents

@m_class{m-noindent}

Three Amiga systems are supported, and one thing they share decides the shape of all of them: none of
them has graphics hardware the game's *shader* pipeline can use. The classic machines have no texturing
or blending at all @m_span{m-text m-dim} (RTG cards accelerate blits and fills, nothing that helps here)
@m_endspan, so there the CPU software rasterizer @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`Software` RHI, which the
desktop and Vita builds already ship) @m_endspan is not a fallback but the renderer.

The two PowerPC systems do have 3D hardware, and what each of them offers the game is a **fixed-function
OpenGL 1.x** - TinyGL on MorphOS, MiniGL over Warp3D on AmigaOS 4. That is exactly the shape the game's
console backends target, so both are driven by the `LegacyGL` rendering backend
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref amiga-morphos-gl) @m_endspan, which is the default on both -- `Software`
stays selectable for a machine whose card has no 3D driver. Everything below that differs:

System | Toolchain @m_span{m-text m-dim} (CMake toolchain file) @m_endspan | Renderer | Window/input | Audio | Details
------ | -------------------------------------------------------------- | -------- | ------------ | ----- | -------
AmigaOS 3.x @m_span{m-text m-dim} (m68k) @m_endspan | AmigaPorts' m68k-amigaos-gcc @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`amiga.cmake`) @m_endspan | `Software` | own backend @m_span{m-text m-dim} (Intuition + RTG) @m_endspan | `AHI` | @ref amiga-classic
AmigaOS 4.1 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (PowerPC) @m_endspan | adtools @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`os4.cmake`) @m_endspan | `LegacyGL` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (MiniGL) @m_endspan | SDL2 | `SDL` | @ref amiga-os4
MorphOS @m_span{m-text m-dim} (PowerPC) @m_endspan | MorphOS SDK @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`morphos.cmake`) @m_endspan | `LegacyGL` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (TinyGL) @m_endspan | SDL2 | `SDL` | @ref amiga-morphos

@m_class{m-noindent}

The design document behind all three, including the hardware research the classic port is based on, is
`Docs/AmigaPortDesign.md` in the repository.

@section amiga-classic AmigaOS 3.x (m68k)

The classic Amiga build is a native **AmigaOS 3.x/m68k** port that covers the whole fast-Amiga spectrum
from a single executable: PiStorm/Emu68 accelerators, Apollo Vampire boards and accelerated 68060
machines with an RTG graphics card. The rasterizer presents into a Picasso96/CyberGraphX chunky
framebuffer, and what differs between machines is not the code path but the *size* of the work, which
is chosen at startup by measuring the machine — see @ref amiga-presets.

Property | Value
-------- | -----
Toolchain | AmigaPorts' **m68k-amigaos-gcc** @m_span{m-text m-dim} (GCC 16.2, prebuilt release; `cmake/toolchains/amiga.cmake`) @m_endspan
C runtime | libnix @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`-mcrt=nix20`) @m_endspan
Rendering backend | `Software` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (pinned -- any other value is a configure error) @m_endspan, forced to 16-bit surfaces
Window/input backend | `Amiga` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`Sources/nCine/Backends/Amiga/`) @m_endspan — Intuition screen, IDCMP input, lowlevel.library gamepads
Audio backend | `AHI` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`Sources/nCine/Audio/Backends/Amiga/`) @m_endspan — software mixer into `ahi.device`
Music | **libxmp** @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`NCINE_WITH_XMP`, downloaded and built by the CMake configure) @m_endspan, reads the original `.j2b` modules directly
Build artifact | `build/amiga/dist/` — the `Jazz2` executable next to its `Content/` tree

@subsection amiga-hardware What it runs on

The port targets machines that are, in CPU terms, at or above the consoles the game already runs on.
The table below is the honest picture, not a compatibility list.

Machine | Verdict
------- | -------
PiStorm / Emu68 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (Pi3A+, CM4, Pi4) @m_endspan | **The primary target.** Dreamcast-class or better, RTG framebuffer only — exactly what the software renderer wants.
Apollo Vampire V4 / V1200 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (68080 + SAGA) @m_endspan | Supported, with the AMMX scanline kernels enabled automatically. Expect reductions rather than a full-resolution game.
68060 + RTG card @m_span{m-text m-dim} (Picasso IV, ZZ9000, …) @m_endspan | The floor. This is the `Low` preset — heavily reduced and experimental.
68030/68040, or any machine without an FPU | No. The build refuses to start without an FPU, and the update loop is floating point throughout.
Stock AGA machines @m_span{m-text m-dim} (no RTG) @m_endspan | Not supported. A chunky-to-planar demake tier is discussed in the design document as a stretch goal, and is not part of this build.

@m_class{m-noindent}

An RTG screen mode is required: the port opens a CyberGraphX/Picasso96 screen and presents into it.
@m_span{m-text m-dim} At least 8 MB of fast RAM is realistic -- the game itself asks for far more than
a stock machine has. @m_endspan

@subsection amiga-toolchain Toolchain

The cross-compiler is [AmigaPorts/m68k-amigaos-gcc](https://github.com/AmigaPorts/m68k-amigaos-gcc),
which is **published as a prebuilt release** for Linux x86_64 and macOS arm64 -- so no cross-compiler
has to be built. The releases are GCC 16.2; GCC 13 is the floor, this codebase being C++17 throughout.

@code{.sh}
# The archive's own directory IS the prefix - the toolchain is relocatable, so unpack it anywhere
curl -fsSLO https://github.com/AmigaPorts/m68k-amigaos-gcc/releases/download/v16.2-rc6/m68k-amigaos-gcc-16.2-rc6-Linux-x86_64.tar.xz
tar xJf m68k-amigaos-gcc-16.2-rc6-Linux-x86_64.tar.xz -C /opt
export AMIGA_INST=/opt/m68k-amigaos-gcc-16.2
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

The release already carries everything this port compiles against except one library. **NDK 3.2**, the
**CyberGraphX and AHI headers** @m_span{m-text m-dim} (which the NDK itself does not include) @m_endspan
and **vasm** @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`vasmm68k_mot`, needed only for `NCINE_WITH_AMMX` -- the AMMX
kernels are hand-written assembly that no GCC can emit) @m_endspan are all in the archive. What is
left is **zlib**, the one third-party library the game cannot run without:

@code{.sh}
export PATH="$AMIGA_INST/bin:$PATH"
curl -LO https://zlib.net/zlib-1.3.1.tar.gz && tar xf zlib-1.3.1.tar.gz && cd zlib-1.3.1
CC=m68k-amigaos-gcc AR=m68k-amigaos-ar RANLIB=m68k-amigaos-ranlib \
    CFLAGS='-mcpu=68060 -O2 -mcrt=nix20' ./configure --prefix=$AMIGA_INST/m68k-amigaos --static
make -j$(nproc) libz.a && make install
@endcode

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par AR and RANLIB have to be named explicitly
Left at their defaults, zlib's configure uses the **host** `ar`/`ranlib`, and the host `ranlib`
writes an ELF-shaped symbol index into the Amiga-format archive. `m68k-amigaos-ld` cannot read that
index, so `libz.a` contributes no symbols at all and the game's link fails on `crc32`, `inflate*`
and `deflate*` -- with the archive sitting right there on the command line. Running
`m68k-amigaos-ranlib libz.a` afterwards repairs an archive already built the wrong way.

@subsubsection amiga-toolchain-flags What the toolchain file sets, and why

`cmake/toolchains/amiga.cmake` is small, but three of its flags are load-bearing and must not be
"cleaned up":

<ul>
<li>`-mcpu=68060` **with** an explicit `-m68881`. The CPU flag alone does *not* imply the FPU ---
`m68k-amigaos-gcc -mcpu=68060 -Q --help=target` reports `-msoft-float [enabled]` --- so without
`-m68881` every floating-point operation in the update loop compiles into a libgcc call. Measured
under an emulated 68040: **1550 ns** per multiply-add soft, **300 ns** with the FPU, the integer
side of the same benchmark unchanged. It is also an ABI trap, because the libnix `libm` the link
resolves to @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`libm020/libm881/libm.a`) @m_endspan is the hard-float one either
way: it returns floating point in `fp0`, and soft-float code reads the return value from `d0`. That
mismatch is what made `fminf()`, `fmaxf()`, `rint()` and `nearbyint()` return garbage
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (`round()` and `trunc()` appeared to work only because their result bits are
left in `d0` as well) @m_endspan. `-print-multi-directory` reports `.` for this flag combination,
which looks alarming, but the link resolves the `libm020/libm881` variant of every library --- which
is what actually matters.</li>
<li>`-fno-optimize-sibling-calls` — this is **correctness, not tuning**. The m68k-amigaos binutils
resolves a sibling call @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`bra.l`, and `ld`'s own `60FF`/`61FF` → `4EB9`
conversion) @m_endspan to a *weak* symbol against the wrong address, so any tail call into an inline
or template function jumps into garbage. Ordinary `jsr` calls to the same symbols are fine, which is
why the mitigation is this narrow. `-fno-weak` is **not** an alternative: it also removes weak
*data*, and template statics such as `Vector2f::Zero` then fail to link.</li>
<li>`-std=gnu++17`, pinned rather than left to CMake. `target_compile_features(cxx_std_17)` is a
*minimum*, so on a compiler that already defaults higher -- GCC 16 defaults to `gnu++20` -- CMake
adds no `-std` flag at all and this target alone would build as C++20. Beyond the inconsistency, the
toolchain's `<math.h>` pulls `using std::lerp;` into the global namespace under C++20, which is
ambiguous against the engine's own `nCine::lerp` at every call site.</li>
</ul>

@m_class{m-noindent}

The toolchain also force-includes `cmake/toolchains/amiga-libstdc++-c99.h` into every C++ translation
unit, the same arrangement the PlayStation 3 build uses: the m68k-amigaos libstdc++ is configured
without `_GLIBCXX_USE_C99_MATH_TR1`, so `<cmath>` declares none of the C99 maths set inside `std::`
even though the library provides it globally.

@subsection amiga-content Preparing the content tree

Unlike the consoles that play from read-only media, an Amiga has somewhere to write: `Source` and
`Cache` are resolved next to the executable, so an original *Jazz Jackrabbit 2* installation copied
into `Source` is converted on the machine on the first start, exactly as on the desktop. It is only
*slow* --- the conversion decodes every animation, tileset and level and writes a compressed package
out of them, which is minutes of work on a desktop CPU and a great deal more on a 68060 --- so the
build is normally pointed at a **prebaked** tree produced once on the host with
@ref asset-packer "AssetPacker" instead:

@code{.sh}
cmake -B ./build/amiga/ -D CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=./cmake/toolchains/amiga.cmake \
    -D CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -D NCINE_CONTENT_DIR=./build/ConsoleContent
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

Point `NCINE_CONTENT_DIR` at the repository's `Content` directory instead and the build carries only
the base assets --- the game then starts and shows the "content missing" message until an original
installation is placed in `Source` next to it. Neither route has been timed on real hardware yet.

@subsection amiga-build Building

@code{.sh}
export AMIGA_INST=/opt/amiga
export PATH="$AMIGA_INST/bin:$PATH"

cmake -B ./build/amiga/ -D CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=./cmake/toolchains/amiga.cmake \
    -D CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -D NCINE_CONTENT_DIR=./build/ConsoleContent
cmake --build ./build/amiga/ --parallel $(nproc)
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

`AMIGA_INST` is the toolchain prefix, and it is checked before anything is configured: the toolchain
file wants `$AMIGA_INST/bin/m68k-amigaos-gcc` and the NDK headers to be there, with zlib installed
beside them @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref amiga-toolchain) @m_endspan. The
`AmigaOS 3.x` job in `.github/workflows/amiga.yml` is the same recipe end to end -- download the
release, cross-compile zlib, configure, build -- if an unattended one is what is wanted.

The build stages `build/amiga/dist/`, which is the directory to copy to the Amiga: the `Jazz2`
executable with its `Content/` tree next to it. `Jazz2.config` is written into that same directory,
which is the conventional home of a program's settings on AmigaOS.

Two notes specific to this target. **Link-time optimization is off** @m_span{m-text m-dim}
(`NCINE_LINKTIME_OPTIMIZATION`) @m_endspan and Release compiles at `-O2` rather than `-O3`, for
the same reason as the consoles: this is the least-tested compiler in the project and code size
matters. And **threads are off** — there is no `std::thread` on m68k-amigaos — so everything,
including audio decoding, runs on the main thread.

@subsection amiga-deploy Deploying and running

On real hardware, copy `dist/` anywhere on a hard drive and run the executable from a Shell or its
icon. It opens its own screen and closes it on exit. The stack is set from the executable's own
`__stack` cookie @m_span{m-text m-dim} (1 MB) @m_endspan, so no `Stack` command is needed, but a
`Stack 1048576` before it does no harm if you start it from a script.

For development the port is exercised under **FS-UAE with AROS 68k**, whose open-source Kickstart
replacement ships CyberGraphX and AHI:

@code{.ini}
# jazz2.fs-uae - AROS boots to Workbench in about ten seconds like this
amiga_model = A4000
cpu = 68040
jit_compiler = 0
kickstart_file = /path/to/aros/boot/amiga/aros-rom.bin
hard_drive_0 = /path/to/aros/dh0
hard_drive_1 = /path/to/jazz2/build/amiga/dist
@endcode

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par AROS specifics that cost an afternoon each
The AROS 68k ROM **hangs before video** with `cpu = 68060` and with the JIT enabled — use an
emulated 68040 and `jit_compiler = 0`. Its `Startup-Sequence` never runs `AddAudioModes`, so the AHI
mode database is empty and *nothing on the system can make a sound* until you add
`C:AddAudioModes DEVS:AudioModes/PAULA` to `S/User-Startup` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (AmigaDOS patterns
like `DEVS:AudioModes/#?` are not expanded for this command) @m_endspan. And FS-UAE's built-in
`kickstart_file = internal` AROS is far too old for a current AROS installation to boot on.

@m_class{m-noindent}

The game writes its log to standard output unbuffered, so redirecting it to a file on the emulated
disk gives a complete trace even after a crash:

@code
Jazz2:Jazz2 >Jazz2:jazz2-log.txt
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

A build configured with `-DDEATH_DEBUG=ON` also accepts `/level <episode>/<level>`, which boots
straight into a level and is by far the fastest way to exercise the renderer:

@code
Jazz2:Jazz2 /level prince/01_castle1 >Jazz2:jazz2-log.txt
@endcode

@subsection amiga-presets Performance presets and autodetection

There is no configuration to get right before the game starts. `AmigaPlatform::RunPerformanceProbe()`
times a 16-bit blend loop and an FPU dependency chain at startup and picks one of four presets, which
then drive the screen mode and the audio mixing rate:

Preset | Pixel budget @m_span{m-text m-dim} (screen mode is chosen to fit) @m_endspan | Mixing rate
------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -----------
`Ultra` | 640×512 | 44100 Hz
`High` | 640×360 | 44100 Hz
`Medium` | 384×300 | 22050 Hz
`Low` | 320×220 | 22050 Hz

@m_class{m-noindent}

The screen mode is not requested by number: the display database is searched for the RTG mode whose
*logical* resolution @m_span{m-text m-dim} (the 720×405-clamped aspect fit the game renders at)
@m_endspan is largest within the preset's pixel budget. That is why a 640×400 mode is chosen over a
640×480 one where both exist — it yields more logical pixels for less work.

Two environment variables override what the machine reports, which is what you want when bringing the
port up on a new accelerator:

Variable | Effect
-------- | ------
`NCINE_PRESET` | `low`, `medium`, `high` or `ultra` — forces the preset instead of measuring it.
`NCINE_NO_AMMX` | Set to anything to keep the scalar scanline kernels on an Apollo 68080. The AMMX kernels are written to be bit-identical to the scalar ones @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`SwBackendHarness` verifies that on hardware) @m_endspan, so this is a diagnostic, not a workaround anyone should need.

@subsection amiga-limits Limits and known issues

<ul>
<li>**An FPU is required, and on a 68040/68060 so is its support library.** The binary is compiled
for hardware floating point @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`-mcpu=68060 -m68881`) @m_endspan and the game's
update loop is floating point throughout, so it exits with a message rather than trapping on a
machine without an FPU at all. Neither the 68040 nor the 68060 implements the whole 68881 set in
silicon, though --- `FMOVECR` and the extended-precision forms GCC emits are left to Motorola's FPSP
--- so `Libs:68040.library` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (resp. `68060.library`) @m_endspan has to be
installed for `SetPatch` to load. Every accelerator ships it and Workbench 3.5/3.9 include it, but a
bare Workbench 3.1 does **not**, and the symptom there is an immediate `Recoverable Alert
0006 8881` rather than anything the game gets to report. The Apollo 68080 and Emu68 implement the
instructions natively and need nothing.</li>
<li>**RTG only.** There is no chunky-to-planar path, so an AGA-only machine cannot run this build.</li>
<li>**No threads.** `WITH_THREADS` is off, so audio streaming decodes synchronously on the main
thread and the multiplayer client/server are not built.</li>
<li>**Music is libxmp, not libopenmpt.** libopenmpt's mixer is written for machines two orders of
magnitude faster than a 68060. libxmp reads the original `.j2b` Galaxy modules directly, so the
soundtrack needs no conversion - what it cannot read is **MO3**, so the four `.mo3` tracks are
silent here @m_span{m-text m-dim} (the PSP shares both facts, see @ref consoles-psp-limits)
@m_endspan. The library is downloaded and built by the CMake configure
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (`cmake/Findlibxmp.cmake`) @m_endspan rather than vendored.</li>
<li>**`ahi.device` implementations disagree about queueing.** The AHI autodocs describe a queue in
which a request carrying `ahir_Link` starts the moment the one before it finishes -- AROS 68k's device
accepts such a request and then never plays or replies to it, and it also stops servicing a queue
that has run dry — which one long level-loading frame is enough to cause. The backend therefore
measures linking at startup with two throwaway requests of its own and restarts a queue that has not
retired anything for a second. Both mechanisms are inert on a device that behaves as documented -- the
log line `Audio device initialized: …` states which mode is in use.</li>
<li>**libnix does not ship every C99 function.** `exp2`, `exp2f`, `log2f`, `strnlen` and `wcstoul`
are missing entirely and are defined in `Sources/nCine/Backends/Amiga/AmigaLibcCompat.c`, along with
the POSIX entry points AmigaOS has no equivalent for @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`flock`, `fchmod`,
`getpwuid`, `realpath`, `gethostname`) @m_endspan and `__xpg_strerror_r`, which libstdc++ references
by name. What is *not* in that file any more is a replacement for `round`, `trunc`, `fmin`, `fmax`,
`rint` or `nearbyint`: those returned garbage under the old soft-float flags and are correct as
libnix ships them once the build is hard-float @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref
amiga-toolchain-flags) @m_endspan.</li>
<li>**Emulator timings are worth exactly one thing: A/B comparisons.** FS-UAE interpreting a 68040
runs gameplay at well under one frame per second, and its *absolute* numbers say nothing about a
PiStorm --- but they are stable and they scale with the code, which is enough to compare two builds
of the same thing. The startup probe is the instrument: it prints `… ns/composited pixel, …
ns/FPU multiply-add` into the log on every boot, and the soft-float regression above was found by
reading the second of those two numbers. Note that the integer figure it reports under emulation
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (around 330 ns/pixel here) @m_endspan happens to land in the same band as a
real 68060/50, while the emulated FPU does not --- so the two halves of that line are not comparable
with each other. Anything that is not a comparison between two builds belongs on hardware.</li>
</ul>

@section amiga-os4 AmigaOS 4.1 (PowerPC)

AmigaOS 4 needs no backend of its own: it has a maintained SDL2, so the window and input side is the
SDL2 backend the desktop builds use, and audio is the `SDL` backend @m_span{m-text m-dim}
(`Sources/nCine/Audio/Backends/SDL/`) @m_endspan - the same software mixer as the classic Amiga's,
handing its blocks to `SDL_QueueAudio()` instead of to `ahi.device`, because the AmigaOS 4.1 SDK has
no OpenAL.

@subsection amiga-os4-gl Hardware rendering through MiniGL

The renderer is the `LegacyGL` backend on **MiniGL**, the SDK's fixed-function OpenGL over Warp3D
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (`libGL.a` plus `minigl.library` -- SDL2's own `SDL_opengl.h` includes its headers
on this platform) @m_endspan. It is the same backend MorphOS uses for TinyGL, so
@ref amiga-morphos-gl describes what it does -- two things differ here:

<ul>
<li>**MiniGL has the texture combiners** it needs - it advertises `GL_EXT_texture_env_combine`, whose
enumerants are the ARB ones under `_EXT` names @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`GL_COMBINE_EXT` is `GL_COMBINE`
is `0x8570`) @m_endspan - but it has **no framebuffer objects at all**, not even entry points to call.
So the render-target path is not probed here the way it is on TinyGL: the copy-back form is compiled
in and the framebuffer-object one is compiled out (`RHI_LEGACYGL_HAS_FBO` in `LegacyGlApi.h`).</li>
<li>**Mirrored repeat is not offered**, so that wrap mode degrades to a plain repeat rather than being
sent to a driver that would answer `GL_INVALID_ENUM` and leave the sampler as it was.</li>
</ul>

@m_class{m-noindent}

The other road to this hardware is Warp3D Nova's **ogles2.library**, which the engine's ES2 profile
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (`NCINE_RHI_GL_PROFILE=ES2` - the one the PS Vita's vitaGL build uses) @m_endspan
would match - that would be the *shader* pipeline rather than this fixed-function one. What is missing
is not engine support but the SDK: it carries no GLES2 headers or link library for it. `Software`
remains selectable @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`-D NCINE_PREFERRED_RHI=Software`) @m_endspan for a machine
with no 3D driver installed -- the CPU on these machines is PC-class, so it is a real option rather than
a compromise.

@subsection amiga-os4-build Toolchain and building

The cross-compiler is **adtools** @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`ppc-amigaos-gcc` 11.5) @m_endspan together
with the AmigaOS 4.1 SDK. Both come in one maintained container image, so nothing has to be
installed - run the build inside it with the repository mounted at its own path:

@code{.sh}
docker run --rm -v "$PWD:$PWD" -w "$PWD" docker.io/walkero/amigagccondocker:os4-gcc11 bash -lc '
    cmake -B ./build/os4/ -D CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=./cmake/toolchains/os4.cmake \
        -D CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -D NCINE_CONTENT_DIR=./build/ConsoleContent
    cmake --build ./build/os4/ --parallel $(nproc)'
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

@m_span{m-text m-dim} (Podman takes the same arguments -- add `--security-opt label=disable` where
SELinux is enforcing.) @m_endspan The toolchain file expects the adtools prefix at the container's
`/opt/ppc-amigaos` -- set `OS4_INST` to build against a host installation instead. Either way the
result is staged into `build/os4/dist/` - the `Jazz2` executable next to its `Content/` tree, copied
to the machine as it is. The executable is stripped on the way: this toolchain leaves DWARF in even
in a Release build, which is two thirds of its size.

The same two commands run in CI, in the container job of `.github/workflows/amiga.yml`.

@subsection amiga-os4-limits What differs from a desktop build

<ul>
<li>**No online multiplayer.** The bsdsocket stack here is IPv4-only - there is no `<netinet/in6.h>`
and no `struct in6_addr` in the SDK - while the bundled ENet is built around IPv6 addresses with
IPv4-mapped ones inside them. The WebSocket transport is out for a smaller reason: IXWebSocket
includes `<poll.h>`, and the SDK has none @m_span{m-text m-dim} (the OS offers `select()` and
`WaitSelect()`) @m_endspan. Local splitscreen is unaffected.</li>
<li>**Threads are on**, unlike the classic Amiga and MorphOS, but the engine's synchronisation layer
calls the pthread API directly and this toolchain's `-athread=pthread` flavour cannot be used - its
`gthr-amigaos-pthread.o` is not shipped. The build therefore uses `-athread=native` for the compiler's
gthreads and links the SDK's own `libpthread`. Thread affinity, thread names, barriers and
`pthread_kill()` are all missing from that library, so those paths are compiled out.</li>
<li>**`libatomic` is linked explicitly**: this is a 32-bit PowerPC, so a 64-bit atomic load is a
library call rather than an instruction.</li>
<li>**`-Uamiga`** is part of the machine flags. The compiler predefines both `AMIGA` and a lowercase
`amiga`, the way ancient compilers predefined `unix`, and the lowercase one breaks any C++ with an
identifier of that name - libopenmpt has `common_encoding::amiga`.</li>
</ul>

@section amiga-morphos MorphOS (PowerPC)

MorphOS is built the same way as AmigaOS 4 - SDL2 for window and input, the `SDL` audio backend, and
the same `LegacyGL` renderer on the system's own fixed-function OpenGL @m_span{m-text m-dim}
(@ref amiga-morphos-gl) @m_endspan - with a different toolchain and a different set of system quirks.
What TinyGL does not have is shaders, which is why that backend is a fixed-function one rather than the
engine's OpenGL backend.

@subsection amiga-morphos-build Toolchain and building

The MorphOS SDK @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`ppc-morphos-gcc` 11.3, at `/gg`) @m_endspan comes as a
container image as well. One thing has to be built before the game itself: the SDK ships **no zlib**,
and the game cannot run without it @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`PakFile`) @m_endspan.

@code{.sh}
docker run --rm -v "$PWD:$PWD" -w "$PWD" -e MORPHOS_DEPS=/tmp/morphos-deps \
    docker.io/walkero/amigagccondocker:mos-gcc-amd64 bash -lc '
    curl -fsSL https://github.com/madler/zlib/releases/download/v1.3.1/zlib-1.3.1.tar.gz | tar xz -C /tmp
    cd /tmp/zlib-1.3.1
    CC=ppc-morphos-gcc CFLAGS="-O2 -noixemul" ./configure --prefix=$MORPHOS_DEPS --static
    make -j $(nproc) libz.a && make install

    cd '"$PWD"'
    cmake -B ./build/morphos/ -D CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=./cmake/toolchains/morphos.cmake \
        -D CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -D NCINE_CONTENT_DIR=./build/ConsoleContent
    cmake --build ./build/morphos/ --parallel $(nproc)'
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

`MORPHOS_DEPS` is where the toolchain file looks for cross-compiled dependencies -- `MORPHOS_SDK`
points at an SDK somewhere other than `/gg`. The result is staged into `build/morphos/dist/`, and
`.github/workflows/amiga.yml` runs these same steps in CI.

@m_class{m-block m-info}

@par Audio: OpenAL where it is installed, SDL otherwise
The SDK ships no OpenAL either, and unlike zlib it is optional: the build looks for one and falls
back to the `SDL` audio backend @m_span{m-text m-dim} (the software mixer feeding `SDL_QueueAudio()`)
@m_endspan when there is none, which is what the CI build gets. To use OpenAL instead, cross-compile
BSzili's port of openal-soft @m_span{m-text m-dim} (its AHI backend -- on MorphOS Storage, and its own
sources rather than the prebuilt library, which was linked against an older C runtime) @m_endspan
into the same `MORPHOS_DEPS` prefix before configuring.

@subsection amiga-morphos-run Running it, and what has to be installed first

MorphOS ships no SDL2 - the SDK's `libSDL2.a` is a stub that opens **`sdl2.library`** at load time, and
that library is a separate download @m_span{m-text m-dim} ("SDL 2.32.11 Libraries" on MorphOS Storage,
which also carries sdl2_image/mixer/ttf) @m_endspan. Without it in `LIBS:` the game does not start at
all: the loader puts up a requester before `main()` runs. Nothing else has to be installed - the port
deliberately does not depend on a TCP/IP stack @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see the note about
bsdsocket.library below) @m_endspan.

The port can be tested without MorphOS hardware, because QEMU emulates the Pegasos II that MorphOS
runs on, and its built-in open firmware boots the MorphOS ISO directly:

@code{.sh}
# boot.img is extracted from the top level of the MorphOS ISO
qemu-system-ppc -machine pegasos2 -m 1024 -rtc base=localtime     -device ati-vga,romfile="" -cdrom morphos-3.20.iso -kernel boot.img -serial stdio
@endcode

@m_class{m-block m-info}

@par Driving it headlessly
Add `-display none -serial file:serial.log -qmp unix:qmp.sock,server,nowait` and the machine can be
run entirely from scripts: `screendump` over QMP takes screenshots, and the game's own log reaches
`serial.log` if it is started with its output redirected to `DEBUG:`, which MorphOS routes to the
serial port. Two things are worth knowing before trying it. The live CD stops at a first-boot
language requester that is put up by `IPrefs`, and *nothing after that point in the startup-sequence
runs until it is answered* - so an automated test has to hook itself in **before** `IPrefs`, where
`LIBS:` and `FONTS:` are not assigned yet and have to be assigned by hand. And a remastered ISO is
only recognised as bootable if its **System Id** is `MORPHOS` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`xorriso -as
mkisofs -sysid MORPHOS -V MorphOSBoot …`) @m_endspan.

@subsection amiga-morphos-gl Hardware rendering through TinyGL

MorphOS' 3D interface is **TinyGL** @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`tinygl.library`, part of the system on
machines whose graphics card it supports - Radeon, Voodoo, Permedia) @m_endspan. It is an OpenGL 1.x
with the 1.3 texture combiners, framebuffer objects and vertex arrays, and no programmable stage at
all. That is exactly the shape the game's console backends target, so it is served by a backend of the
same family rather than by the engine's OpenGL one:

It is the default on both PowerPC Amigas, so an ordinary build already uses it -- the CPU rasterizer is
one option away for a machine whose graphics card has no 3D driver:

@code{.sh}
cmake -B ./build/morphos/ -D CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=./cmake/toolchains/morphos.cmake \
    -D CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -D NCINE_PREFERRED_RHI=Software
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

`LegacyGL` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`Sources/nCine/Graphics/RHI/LegacyGL/`) @m_endspan is a
fixed-function backend in the same sense as the Dreamcast's PowerVR or the PSP's GE one: it consumes
the same transpiled `fixed_function` effect tables @m_span{m-text m-dim}
(`Shaders/Generated/LegacyGlGeneratedEffects.h`) @m_endspan and expresses each effect's texture stage
as a `GL_COMBINE` program, so the game runs the *direct tier* - the scene goes straight to the display
at the logical resolution and the CPU lightmap is composited by the device instead of a shader pass.
Three things about it are worth knowing:

<ul>
<li>**Indexed textures are baked, not palettised.** The consoles resolve an index through hardware
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (CLUT, TLUT, palette bank) @m_endspan -- legacy GL has no such thing left
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (`GL_EXT_paletted_texture` is gone from every driver and TinyGL never had it)
@m_endspan, so every indexed image is baked through its palette row into RGBA8 before it is
uploaded.</li>
<li>**Textures are padded to powers of two where the GL insists on them.** Sampling a
non-power-of-two texture is a GL 2.0 feature, so both that and `GL_MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE` are probed at
startup @m_span{m-text m-dim} (the reported limit is capped at 2048 - a desktop driver answers 16384,
which is not a size anything here should build) @m_endspan. Tileset atlases are laid out 15 tiles to a
row and chunked 512 texels tall, exactly as on the fixed-function consoles, so a chunk is one texture
whether or not the padding applies -- anything larger that is prebaked is split into pages internally
rather than rejected. `NCINE_NO_NPOT` in the environment forces the padded path.</li>
<li>**Render targets prefer a framebuffer object and fall back to copying.** Whether FBOs work is
probed at startup rather than assumed - TinyGL declares the entry points unconditionally - and where
they do not, an off-screen pass is drawn into the back buffer and lifted into its texture with
`glCopyTexSubImage2D()`, which is a GL 1.1 operation. Setting `NCINE_NO_FBO` in the environment forces
that path, which is how it can be exercised on a desktop.</li>
</ul>

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par Not runnable under emulation
The `LegacyGL` build does **not** start under the QEMU setup described above, and cannot be made to:
QEMU's ATI card fails its DMA/CCE initialization @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`Radeon: Failed to initialise
Radeon DMA` on the boot console) @m_endspan and `tinygl.library` is then not available at all - a
program that needs it is stopped by the loader with a requester before `main()`. So the default build
cannot be tested there: **build with `-D NCINE_PREFERRED_RHI=Software` for the emulator**, which is
also the configuration that has actually been run on this system. The `LegacyGL` backend itself is
developed and verified against a desktop OpenGL, where a compatibility context runs the very same 1.3
combiner pipeline @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`cmake -DNCINE_PREFERRED_RHI=LegacyGL` on Linux/Windows)
@m_endspan.

@subsection amiga-morphos-limits What differs from a desktop build

The MorphOS SDK's headers are the C-era Amiga ones, and three of their habits collide with a modern
C++ codebase. All three are handled in `cmake/toolchains/morphos.cmake` and
`cmake/toolchains/morphos-compat.h`, and they are worth knowing before touching that file:

<ul>
<li>**The `ppcinline` headers turn OS calls into function-like macros**, and some of those names are
ordinary C++ identifiers. `bind` is the fatal one: `<unistd.h>` pulls in `proto/socket.h`
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (zlib.h includes `<unistd.h>`, so this reaches almost every file) @m_endspan and
the macro then rewrites `std::bind` inside libstdc++'s own `<functional>`. The build defines
`_NO_PPCINLINE`, which keeps the prototypes and drops the macros, and force-includes
`morphos-compat.h` for what that leaves undeclared @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`FD_ZERO` expands to
`bzero`) @m_endspan.</li>
<li>**`proto/exec.h` and `proto/usergroup.h` put `struct Task` in the global namespace**, which is
also the name of the engine's coroutine type - every unqualified `Task<...>` in the game then becomes
ambiguous. Both are skipped by defining their include guards -- `FindTask()`, which `getpid()` is a
macro over, is declared by hand in `morphos-compat.h` with an opaque return type so that `Task` stays
out of the global namespace.</li>
<li>**Threads are off** for the same reason, and this one cannot be worked around: MorphOS's
`<pthread.h>` includes `<exec/semaphores.h>`, so merely using pthreads brings `struct Task` back. The
port runs single-threaded, exactly like the classic Amiga - SDL2's own headers are clean, so nothing
else is affected.</li>
<li>**`INT32_MIN` and friends** are still behind `__STDC_LIMIT_MACROS` here, the pre-C++11 rule, so
the toolchain asks for them explicitly.</li>
<li>`realpath()`, `fsync()`, `flock()` and `wcstoul()` are declared by the C library and implemented
by none of it -- the first three are compiled out and `wcstoul` is provided in
`Sources/nCine/Backends/MorphOS/MorphOSLibcCompat.cpp`, along with a definition of libstdc++'s
`__throw_bad_array_new_length` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (GCC 11 emits calls to it, the SDK's libstdc++
predates it) @m_endspan.</li>
<li>**TinyGL is reached through two globals in the program**, `TinyGLBase` and `__tglContext`, because
the SDK's `gl*` names are macros that read them @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`GLFoo(__tglContext, …)`, the
library base supplying the call vector) @m_endspan. sdl2.library cannot fill them in - it is a shared
library and they live in the program - so the `LegacyGL` build does it: the link asks for the SDK's
own TinyGL initializer by name @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`-u _CSTP_init_TinyGLBase`, which nothing would
otherwise pull out of `libGL.a` because SDL2's glue already declares the same global) @m_endspan, and
`Sources/nCine/Backends/MorphOS/MorphOSTinyGl.cpp` points `__tglContext` at the context
`SDL_GL_CreateContext()` returned.</li>
<li>**`gethostname()` and `getpwuid()` are not called here**, and neither is libcurl linked. All three
reach `bsdsocket.library` or `usergroup.library`, and a *reference* to one of those is enough for the
loader to open it before the program starts - so a machine with no TCP/IP stack running would refuse
to start the game, over a host name that only decorates a log line. The update check goes with them --
online multiplayer is off here anyway.</li>
</ul>

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par What has been run, and what has not
**MorphOS has been run**: under QEMU as described above it boots, loads its content from the CD, opens
its SDL2 window, initializes the SDL audio backend at 44.1 kHz and renders the menu correctly - all of
that with the `Software` renderer, which is what the emulator can run. `LegacyGL`, the default on both
PowerPC Amigas, builds and links for both and is verified against a desktop OpenGL, but **neither
system has run it**: MorphOS cannot start it under emulation at all @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see
@ref amiga-morphos-gl) @m_endspan, and **AmigaOS 4 has not been run at all** - that needs AmigaOS 4.1
itself, which is commercial software that is not available here @m_span{m-text m-dim} (QEMU's
`sam460ex` and `amigaone` machines can run it, given the install media) @m_endspan. Treat both
PowerPC halves as ready to test rather than tested.

*/

//###==##====#=====--==~--~=~- --- -- -  -  -   -
}
